IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `eia-core-interpretation`
IMPORT `er6-part-vii-transitional`

-- Employment Insurance Regulations (SOR/96-332), SCHEDULE III, "Interim Provisions (Section 95)": provisions that applied only from
-- the coming into force of the Regulations (30 June 1996) until the provisions they stood in place of came into force (1 January and 5 January 1997).
-- Text: the plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation current to 2026-09-30 (SOR-96-332.txt, lines 2244-2369);
-- an unofficial conversion, not verified against the official text.
--
-- WHAT THEY ARE. Part I adds a section 6.1 (a very low-earning week is not insurable); Part I.1 replaces section 12 (prescribed weeks);
-- Part II replaces sections 19 to 21 (the record of employment), 22 to 24 (earnings and weeks counted), 34 (dependants), the Table to
-- subsection 55(7) and paragraph 93(1)(c). Reg 95(4), (4.1) and (5) say when each applies (er6-part-vii-transitional).
--
-- HOW THEY ARE GATED. Every rule here takes a DAY and answers only when the matching period of reg 95 contains it; outside the period
-- the provision does not operate, which is the law's own answer: FALSE for a yes/no rule, NOTHING for a number or a date (the
-- ordinary provision, in another piece, governs then). The text does not say which day is tested ("applies ... during the period");
-- these rules test the day of the fact in question, the week worked or the day the duty arises (fork F-SCHED3-DAY in NOTES.md).
--
-- WHAT IS TAKEN AS AN INPUT. The maximum weekly insurable earnings (out of range: the Act's Schedule II item 1, the interim section 4(2),
-- piece ei6, which gives $750 and names no year); the Income Tax Act's meanings (related persons, spouse; no piece: not among the
-- inputs); the Act's sections that are named (7(4)(c), 14, 19, 59, 63, 87(3), Parts III and IV) and regs 35 and 36 (pieces ei1, ei2, ei3, er3).
--
-- Not a section of the text, but a finding: item 2 "applies in place of sections 19 to 21" and item 3 "in place of sections 22 to 24" set out
-- only sections 19 and 20, and 22 and 23; there is no interim section 21 or 24. Item 6 replaces "paragraph 93(1)(c)" and sets out a paragraph
-- (c); the reg 93(1) in the text has only paragraphs (a) and (b) (the hours of paragraph (b) now stand where the interim weeks of (c) stood).

§ `Schedule III -- the nouns of the interim provisions`

-- The pay periods that 6.1(2) and item 3 (s 23) tell apart.
-- src:2249 | (2) Where the cash earnings of a person for a pay period described in any of the following paragraphs are paid or payable to the person otherwise than in respect of weeks, subsection (1) does not apply to the employment of that person during any such pay period:
-- src:2250 | (a) a pay period that is a multiple of a week where
-- src:2251 | (i) the person has cash earnings for each week in the pay period, and the cash earnings for the pay period are equal to or exceed an amount that is a like multiple of the amount of cash earnings referred to in subsection (1), or
-- src:2252 | (ii) the person is employed in each week in the pay period and the total number of hours of employment in the pay period is equal to or exceeds a number of hours that is a like multiple of 15;
-- src:2253 | (b) a pay period that is a semi-monthly pay period where
-- src:2254 | (i) the person has cash earnings for each week or part of a week that falls in the pay period, and the cash earnings for the pay period are equal to or exceed an amount that is 2 1/6 times the amount of cash earnings referred to in subsection (1), or
-- src:2255 | (ii) the person is employed in each week or part of a week that falls in the pay period, and the total number of hours of employment in the pay period is 33 hours or more;
-- src:2256 | (c) a pay period that is a monthly pay period where
-- src:2257 | (i) the person has cash earnings for each week or part of a week that falls in the pay period, and the cash earnings for the pay period are equal to or exceed an amount that is 4 1/3 times the amount of cash earnings referred to in subsection (1), or
-- src:2258 | (ii) the person is employed in each week or part of a week that falls in the pay period, and the total number of hours of employment in the pay period is 65 hours or more;
-- src:2259 | (d) a pay period of seven consecutive days that begins on a day other than Sunday, where
-- src:2260 | (i) the person has cash earnings for the pay period and the cash earnings for the pay period are equal to or exceed 20 per cent of the maximum weekly insurable earnings, or
-- src:2261 | (ii) the person is employed in the pay period for a total number of hours that is equal to or exceeds 15 hours; or
-- src:2262 | (e) a pay period that consists of more than one period of seven consecutive days and that begins on a day other than Sunday, where
-- src:2263 | (i) the person has cash earnings for each period of seven consecutive days in the pay period, and the cash earnings for the pay period are equal to or exceed the product obtained by multiplying the amount of cash earnings referred to in subsection (1) by the number of periods of seven consecutive days in the pay period, or
-- src:2264 | (ii) the person is employed in each period of seven consecutive days in the pay period and the total number of hours of employment in the pay period is equal to or exceeds the product obtained by multiplying 15 by the number of periods of seven consecutive days in the pay period.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations Schedule III Part I s 6.1(2) (src:2249-2264)
DECLARE `er6 Pay period` IS ONE OF
    `er6 a multiple of a week` HAS `number of weeks in the pay period` IS A NUMBER
    `er6 a semi-monthly pay period`
    `er6 a monthly pay period`
    `er6 seven consecutive days beginning on a day other than Sunday`
    `er6 more than one period of seven consecutive days beginning on a day other than Sunday` HAS `number of periods of seven consecutive days in the pay period` IS A NUMBER

-- What the person states about the earnings and hours of one pay period.
DECLARE `er6 Earnings and hours in a pay period` HAS
    `cash earnings for the pay period`                                                       IS A NUMBER
    `total number of hours of employment in the pay period`                                  IS A NUMBER
    `cash earnings for each week, part of a week or period of seven days in the pay period`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `employment in each week, part of a week or period of seven days in the pay period`      IS A BOOLEAN

-- Part I.1, s 12(1): the ten ways a week can be a prescribed week.
